Tension-Type Headache
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Bilateral diffuse band-like head pain, Non-throbbing/pressing quality, Unexacerbated by physical activity, Complete absence of severe nausea/vomiting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-048
Reason for Visit: Severe, unremitting, and continuous head pain causing acute distress, severe sleep deprivation, and intense fear of a brain tumor.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient suffering from a severe, continuous 3-day headache unresponsive to oral analgesia, resulting in intense anxiety and functional impairment. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively perform a neurological examination, exclude acute meningitis, increased intracranial pressure, or a space-occupying lesion, and provide immediate pharmacological relief prior to the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head has been in a crushing vice for three days straight, nothing stops the pressure, and I'm terrified it's a brain tumor."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 72-hour history of severe, unremitting, bilateral, and diffuse mild to moderate head pain that has escalated in perceived intensity tonight due to severe sleep deprivation. The pain quality is classically described as a tight, pressing, and severe 'band-like' squeezing sensation around the entire head. The patient explicitly notes a complete absence of severe throbbing, sharp pulsating, or unilateral lateralization. The pain is distinctly unexacerbated by routine physical activity (walking around the house). There is a complete lack of severe prolonged nausea, profuse vomiting, photophobia, or phonophobia. The severe exacerbation tonight correlates directly with acute occupational psychological stress and profound fatigue.
Functional Status: Pacing, visibly exhausted, rubbing the temples and forehead continuously.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound health anxiety and sleep deprivation impacting cognitive function.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, appearing deeply fatigued but not acutely toxic or photophobic.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological physical examination is completely normal (cranial nerves II-XII globally intact, power 5/5, normal symmetrical reflexes, normal tandem gait). Fundoscopy reveals sharp optic disc margins bilaterally, definitively ruling out papilledema (increased intracranial pressure). Neck is supple with absolute absence of meningism (negative Kernig's/Brudzinski's). However, there is pronounced, exquisite pericranial muscle tenderness upon direct, firm palpation of the posterior cervical, temporalis, and frontal muscle groups. Temporal arteries are non-tender with bounding pulses.
Environmental Observations: House is brightly lit and television is on, confirming the absolute lack of clinical photophobia or phonophobia.
Medication Reconciliation: Overusing paracetamol (4g/day) with zero clinical efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tension-Type Headache - Status: Acute severe prolonged episode
Differentials:

Space-Occupying Lesion (Brain Tumor) - Ruled out by the strictly normal neurological examination, normal fundoscopy (no papilledema), and completely stable presentation without progressive morning vomiting or focal deficits.

Meningitis - Ruled out by the complete absence of systemic fever, lack of photophobia, and perfectly supple neck without meningism.

Migraine - Ruled out as the pain is bilateral, continuous, non-throbbing, and completely lacks associated severe nausea, vomiting, or visual auras.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to a severe, prolonged, and unrelenting headache causing profound psychological distress, absolute sleep deprivation, and extreme fear of a catastrophic intracranial pathology. The unremitting nature of the pain necessitated an immediate, hands-on neurological and fundoscopic examination. By definitively confirming intact structural neurology, ruling out papilledema, and actively identifying severe pericranial muscle tension, the physician safely excluded a life-threatening neurological emergency. Delivering this immediate, concrete physical reassurance successfully broke the severe anxiety cycle, preventing a highly distressed and unnecessary emergency department admission for a CT scan, strictly satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral NSAIDs (Naproxen 500mg stat, then 250mg BID) for targeted musculoskeletal anti-inflammatory effect, and a single dose of oral Promethazine 25mg to forcefully break the insomnia cycle tonight. Advised immediate cessation of excess paracetamol to prevent medication-overuse headaches.
Positive Behavioral Change: When working at a desk, set a strict timer to pause every 30 minutes, actively drop the shoulders, and execute 5 slow neck rolls. Releasing the mechanical tension in the trapezius and cervical musculature directly prevents the fascial tightening that wraps around the skull, directly rewarding the patient with a significant reduction in the 'vice-like' head pressure.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad for the neck.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to monitor headache resolution and discuss stress management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a sudden 'thunderclap' headache (worst pain of your life within seconds), a high fever with a stiff neck, sudden blurry or double vision, or weakness in the face or arms.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the completely normal neurological examination today; any development of true focal weakness or visual changes requires immediate reassessment.
Clinical Course: A tension headache is a severe muscle cramp of the thin muscles that wrap around your skull and neck. It is agonizing and relentless, but it causes absolutely zero damage to your brain. It will resolve completely once the muscle tension and sleep deprivation are addressed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you suddenly develop the worst headache of your life in a split second, if you get a high fever and cannot touch your chin to your chest, or if your vision suddenly goes blurry.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The neurological exam and eye exam we just performed prove your brain is perfectly safe; there is no tumor and no pressure inside your skull. The pain is coming from the muscles on the outside of your head clamping down like a tight band due to extreme stress. Standard painkillers are failing because they don't relax the physical muscle cramp.
Actionable Steps: Tonight, apply a hot, wet towel or heating pad to the back of your neck for 20 minutes before bed. The intense, deep heat physically increases blood flow to the cramped neck muscles, directly forcing them to release the tight fascial band wrapping around your skull and immediately reducing the crushing pressure.

